  "disclosure_policy": "Which arms may be cited to a competitor as evidence their sequence is already known: the 8 arms with visibility='public' only - pdb, swissprot, plabdab, plabdab_nano, therasabdab, thpdb, proteinbase_public, uspto. NOT proteinbase_internal (visibility='internal'), and NOT sequence_similarity/competitionseqs.fasta, which is the same private visibility slice of the proteinbase proteins table: both are other submitters' unpublished work. Search them if you want an internal-duplicate flag for the organisers, but never surface them in feedback to a submitter. prescreen_refdb.best_prior_art() enforces this by filtering to PUBLIC_ARMS; do not widen it.",
  "verification_notes": [
    "pdb holds 987,345 mol:protein and 59,278 mol:na chains; `mmseqs databases PDB` does not filter by molecule type. Nucleic-acid chains cannot match a protein query, so the effective protein prior art is 987,345 chains.",
    "The mounted pdb/swissprot/plabdab databases store sequences int-encoded (dbtype flag bit 0x80000 on pdb and swissprot), so the data files are not readable as text; use `mmseqs convert2fasta` to inspect them.",
    "proteinbase_internal is STALE relative to its own source: proteins.fasta holds 2,578 records but the database index holds 1,051. Re-run createdb before relying on it. It is an internal slice and is not public prior art in any case.",
    "Every arm was confirmed searchable by a real query, not just by createdb reporting success - the failure mode this project has already hit once."
  ],
